984:
After the issuance of taxicab company business license, the chief of police shall have the authority to recommend a denial, revocation, suspension or refusal to renew any taxicab company owner's business license as a result of violations of this article or as a result of event, occurrence or circumstances which would make the taxicab company owner ineligible for a taxicab owner's business license in the first issuance.
(1) An application for a license or a license issued under this division may be denied, suspended, revoked or not renewed, as applicable, for due cause as set forth in subsection (3) of this section.
(2) The city commission shall furnish a license holder a notice setting forth the time, place and purpose of a meeting at which they intend to consider the revocation or suspension of a license. After a hearing, if the city commission determines due cause exists, they may suspend or revoke such license, or not renew the license or place the licensee on probation for a maximum of 12 months, with or without conditions, including, without limitation, the levying of civil monetary penalties, not to exceed $500.00.
(3) A license may be denied, suspended, not renewed or revoked for any of the following reasons:
a. Where the applicant furnishes fraudulent or untruthful information or omits information requested in the application for a license;
b. For failure to pay all fees, taxes, penalties or other charges imposed by the provisions of this division and this Code;
c. For failure to maintain all of the general qualifications applicable to the initial issuance of a license;
d. For violation for any part of this division by the license holder, its agents, partners, officers, employees or contractors;
e. Allowing the required insurance coverage to lapse shall result in the automatic suspension of the license until insurance coverage is reinstated and adequate evidence is provided to the chief of police that such coverage is in effect.
(4) License holders under this division are responsible for violations of this division by their vehicle drivers whether such drivers are direct employees or independent contractors;
(5) Licenses issued under this division are not transferrable.
(Code 1977, § 35-32; Ord. No. 01-23, § 1, 11-20-2001)

988:
No driver shall operate a taxicab on the streets of the city without first obtaining and placing in plain view in the interior of his taxicab a driver's permit which shall be issued by the chief of police upon receipt of the following information to be signed and filed by the driver on an application form provided by the city police department:
(1) Name, age and address of driver;
(2) Name and address of the taxicab company under which the driver operates the taxicab. An established taxicab company must hold all required driver permits;
(3) Whether the applicant for a taxicab driver permit has been convicted of any violation or any laws of the state, the United States or any other state, or any ordinance of the city, If there are any such violations, the applicant shall state the full details, including, without limitation, the offense, court, date of disposition and status;
(4) Driver's license number issued by the state and its date of expiration;
(5) A copy of this article shall be provided to each taxicab driver by the city police department at the time a taxicab driver applies for a taxicab driver permit.
(Code 1952, ch. 23, § 11; Code 1977, § 35-44; Ord. No. 01-23, § 1, 11-20-2001)

989:
Before operating a taxicab within the city, a driver shall:
(1) Possess a valid Class I state driver's license;
(2) Be mentally and physically competent to operate a motor vehicle;
(3) Be familiar with the traffic laws and ordinances of the city;
(4) Not have been convicted of a felony unless five years have elapsed since the completion of the felony sentence;
(5) Not have been convicted of, entered a plea of nolo contendere to, or forfeited a bond in lieu of, any sexual or other crime of moral turpitude within past five years immediately prior to the application for a taxicab driver permit;
(6) Not have been classified as a habitual offender by the state department of public safety or any similar department of any other state or of the United States for the five years immediately prior to the application for a taxicab driver permit;
(7) Provide authorization and any information necessary to enable the chief of police or his designee to investigate the driver's background, criminal history, and traffic record;
(8) Be of good character as determined by the aforesaid investigation;
(9) Possess a taxicab driver's permit issued by the city police department;
(10) Be at least 18 years of age.
(Code 1952, ch. 23, § 17; Code 1977, § 35-45; Ord. No. 76-15, § 1, 10-5-1976; Ord. No. 01-23, § 1, 11-20-2001)

996:
The chief of police shall have the power to deny, revoke, suspend or to refuse to renew any driver permit as a result of violations of this article or as a result of any occurrence which would make the taxicab company owner or driver ineligible for a permit in the first issuance, but no such revocation or non-renewal shall be made without first informing the taxicab company owner and the driver of the reasons therefore and without first giving such taxicab company owner or driver an opportunity to respond.
(1) A denial, revocation, non-renewal or suspension or a permit by the chief of police shall be subject to appeal;
(2) An application for a permit or a permit issued under this division may be denied, suspended, revoked or not renewed for due cause as set forth in subsection (4) of this section;
(3) The chief of police or his designee shall furnish a permit holder a notice setting forth the time, place, and purpose of a meeting at which they intend to consider the revocation or suspension of a permit. After a hearing, if the chief of police or his designee determine due cause exists, they may deny, suspend, revoke the permit or place on probation for a maximum of 12 months, with or without conditions, the permit;
(4) A permit may be denied, suspended, revoked or not renewed for any of the following reasons:
a. Where the permit applicant furnishes fraudulent or untruthful information or omits information requested in the application for a permit;
b. For failure to pay all fees, taxes, penalties or other charges imposed by the provisions of this division and this Code;
c. For failure to maintain all or the general qualifications applicable to the initial issuance of a permit;
d. Permits may be suspended or revoked for having three or more moving violations in any 12-month period;
e. Where the applicant furnishes fraudulent or untruthful information or omits information regarding logs;
f. Failure to stop and render aid or leaving the scene of an accident, as required under the laws of the state;
g. Repeated violation of city ordinances, county laws, or state laws which affect the safety of persons traveling on the streets of the city.
(Code 1977, § 35-33; Ord. No. 01-23, § 1, 11-20-2001)

1006:
(a) Food trucks shall locate on privately owned lots zoned C-2 highway commercial, C-4 central business, and MU mixed use districts, with all-weather surface (or gravel) parking areas, accessible by emergency vehicles, and having adequate legally designated off-street parking spaces, subject to the approval of the property owner and subject to the following:
(1) Written notice to the county health department seven days prior to operations begin. Such notice shall state the expected dates of operation and shall specify the physical location of operations.
(2) The food truck shall be positioned at least 50 yards from the customer entrance of an existing restaurant during the food truck's hours of operation;
(3) No food truck shall be operated within 600 feet of any public school in the city limits between the hours of 7:30 a.m. and 3:30 p.m. on days in which schools are actually in session;
(4) The food truck shall be positioned on designated parking spaces and shall not block drive aisles, other access to loading/service areas, or emergency access and fire lanes;
(5) No food truck shall be located within a building setback or landscape buffer;
(6) The food truck must be positioned at least 15 feet away from fire hydrants, driveway entrances, alleys, handicapped parking spaces, tree trunks, and vegetation;
(7) Food trucks shall not be located within 100 feet of gas pumps, underground gas storage tanks, and vent pipes;
(8) Food trucks may not be located in any portion of a parking lot when and where such location would prevent the use of parking spaces during the regular hours of operation of the primary business on the lot, or otherwise interfere in a significant manner with the movement of motor vehicles, unless prior permission to use the parking spots has been provided by the property owner;
(9) Cooking off the vehicle with a grill or smoker is prohibited;
(10) No seating shall be provided;
(11) Vehicle-mounted roll-out awnings are allowed; and
(12) Vehicle must vacate premises by 10:00 p.m. and return to base of operation daily.
(b) Food trucks selling ice cream only shall be allowed to be in residential zoning districts subject to the following:
(1) All traffic laws must be observed and obeyed;
(2) Food truck selling ice cream only cannot block traffic or create traffic congestion;
(3) Food truck selling ice cream only can operate only between the hours of 9:00 a.m. and 8 p.m. on Monday through Saturday and no food truck selling ice cream only can operate in residential zoning districts at any time on Sunday;
(4) The noise level from the food truck selling ice cream only or any sound equipment attracting, or designed to attract, customers must comply with the noise ordinance and must not create a nuisance.
(Ord. No. O17-25, § 1, 11-7-2017)

1018:
(a) Applications for franchises and revocable licenses shall be submitted in such form and be issued on such terms and conditions as the city commission may determine, subject to applicable law.
(b) Any application for a franchise or revocable license shall contain and/or require the following information with respect to the proposed franchise or revocable license and such other information as the city commission shall deem necessary or appropriate:
(1) Applicant's name, address, telephone number, and federal employer identification number; copy of applicant's corporate charter or partnership agreement as applicable; and any trade names (and registrations) used by applicant;
(2) A detailed statement of the corporation or business entity organization of the applicant, including, but not limited to, the following, and to whatever extent required by the city:
a. The names and the residence and business addresses of all officers and directors of the applicant;
b. The names, residence, and business addresses of all persons and entities having any share of the ownership of the applicant and the respective ownership share of each person or entity;
c. The names and address of any parent or subsidiary of the applicant, namely, any other business entity owning or controlling applicant in whole or in part or owned or controlled in whole or in part by the applicant, and a statement describing the nature of any such parent or subsidiary business entity;
d. For purposes of determining whether an applicant has the financial ability to construct and maintain the telecommunications system in a safe manner and in accordance with the city's management policies, rules and regulations with respect to the streets and to the extent not prohibited by law, a detailed and complete financial statement of the applicant, certified by an independent certified public accountant, for the fiscal year immediately preceding the date of the application hereunder, or a letter or other acceptable evidence in writing from a recognized lending institution or funding source, addressed to both the applicant and the commission, setting forth the basis for any conclusions by such lending institution or funding source regarding the applicant's financial ability to construct and maintain the proposed telecommunications system, and a clear statement of its intent as a lending institution or funding source to provide whatever capital shall be required by the applicant to construct and maintain the proposed telecommunications system in the city, or a statement from an independent certified public accountant certifying that the applicant has available sufficient free, net, and uncommitted cash resources to construct and maintain the proposed telecommunications system in the city; and
e. For purposes of determining whether the applicant will construct, maintain, operate, and repair telecommunications system in compliance with the policies, rules and regulations with respect to the streets and in a manner protective of the public health, safety and welfare, the names and addresses for telecommunications systems owned or controlled by the applicant, its parent, and subsidiaries, and the areas served by such systems;
(3) In order to permit the city to manage its streets, a detailed description of the proposed plan of construction and maintenance of the applicant which shall include, but not be limited to, the following:
a. A detailed map indicating all areas proposed to be served, and a proposed time schedule for the installation of all equipment throughout the entire area to be serviced;
b. A detailed, informative, and referenced statement describing the proposed and actual location of facilities and equipment to be used in the streets by the applicant; and
c. For purposes of assisting the city in determining whether the telecommunications system will be constructed in accordance with the city's management policies, rules and regulations with respect to the streets, a detailed statement setting forth the provisions in any and all agreements and undertakings, whether formal or informal, written, oral, or implied, existing or proposed to exist between the applicant and any person, firm, or corporation which materially relate or pertain to such person, firm, or corporation constructing, or assisting the applicant with the construction of, the telecommunications system in the streets; and
(4) To the extent not prohibited by law, any other details, statements, supplementary information, or references pertinent to the subject matter of such application which shall be required or requested by the commission, or by any other provision of law.
(Code 1977, § 35¼-3; Ord. No. 01-12, § I, 7-17-2001)
